<Base title="Does My AC Use R-22? How to Tell and What It Means" description="Air conditioners and heat pumps made before 2010 almost always use R-22 refrigerant, which is no longer produced in the US. How to check and what to do if yours leaks.">
  <section>
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › Guides › R-22</p>
    <h1>Does my air conditioner use R-22?</h1>
    <p class="lede">If your air conditioner or heat pump was made <b>before 2010</b>, it almost certainly uses R-22 refrigerant (sometimes sold as Freon). New systems built from 2010 use R-410A or newer refrigerants. <a href="/air-conditioner-age/">Find your AC's age from the serial number</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>How to check</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Look at the data plate on the outdoor unit: it lists the refrigerant type ("R-22" or "HCFC-22", or "R-410A").</li>
      <li>Decode the serial number. Made before 2010 means R-22 in almost every case. Units made in 2010 can be either, so check the plate.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>The US stopped producing and importing R-22 at the start of 2020 to protect the ozone layer. Systems can still be serviced with recycled or stockpiled R-22, but it is scarce and expensive. If an older R-22 system develops a leak, replacing the system is often better value than recharging it, especially if it is past 12 to 15 years old.</p>
    <p>You don't have to replace a working R-22 system. It just means planning ahead.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
